1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,740
We are in a message series on the book of Esther,

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:09,860
and I want to start with a question for those of you that are followers of Christ,

3
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:12,180
for those of you that are Christians.

4
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:17,340
I wonder, have you ever gotten so comfortable in your life

5
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:22,860
that you stopped depending on God the way that you once did?

6
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:24,700
Some of you may be there today.

7
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:30,900
You want a good life, you pursue a good life, you do it in the name of God, for the glory of God,

8
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:34,860
and then sometimes when you get the very things that you want

9
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:37,820
and you finally start to feel a little bit comfortable,

10
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:44,060
in your comfort, sometimes you actually stop depending on God the way that you once did.

11
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:46,260
This happened to me.

12
00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:49,300
About a decade or so into Life.Church,

13
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:54,780
Amy and I started the church together with a handful of people back in 1996,

14
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:59,980
about a decade into it, things were finally really starting to work and getting a little bit easier.

15
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:03,660
We had a lot of people coming to faith in Christ. We finally had a staff.

16
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:08,220
We had full volunteers. We just, things were working,

17
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:11,540
and I kind of thought, "I'll take a breath."

18
00:01:11,540 --> 00:01:15,620
I got a phone call from a mentor of mine whose name is Lyle Schaller.

19
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:18,500
Lyle is in heaven today.

20
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:23,100
Lyle was one of the, one of the wisest, most godly men that I knew.

21
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:27,500
I got a phone call from Lyle, and after the normal "how you doing" small talk,

22
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,780
Lyle said, "Hey, let me ask you a question."

23
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:35,740
Now, every time Lyle had a question for you, it was going to be a good question.

24
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:39,020
And he said, "Let me ask you a question." And he asked me, he said, "Craig,

25
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:45,900
when is the last time that you made a decision that actually took faith?

26
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:50,020
When is the last time you made a decision that actually took faith?"

27
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:54,740
I kind of bowed up. "Lyle, you're talking to Mr. Faith.

28
00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:57,980
Everything I do requires faith. My whole life is..."

29
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:04,180
I was a little bit, a little bit hurt, a little bit offended. "I'm a pastor, I started this church, I got a lot of faith."

30
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:08,700
And he didn't argue. He just kind of listened to me, and then he asked again. He said, "Well, just be really specific.

31
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:16,380
Give me a very specific time recently that you did something that required faith."

32
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:22,100
And I couldn't answer him, which was embarrassing.

33
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:27,540
Because earlier, in the early years of the church, like everything we did was by faith. I mean everything.

34
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:30,820
Like we started out with no building. We had a borrowed garage,

35
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:37,260
we met in a garage with borrowed chairs, a borrowed sound system. We didn't, we didn't have cloth, we didn't even have a casserole lady.

36
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:41,940
And in the 1990s, every church had a casserole lady. We didn't have it.

37
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:46,900
And it was really, really hard. And somewhere along the way, we started getting the things that we thought that we needed,

38
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:51,540
and I just wanted it to be easier, and it finally was easier. And when it got easier,

39
00:02:51,540 --> 00:02:59,340
I didn't even realize it, but without knowing it, I think I just started to kind of eliminate risk.

40
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:05,980
And when you start to eliminate risk, you don't really need as much faith.

41
00:03:05,980 --> 00:03:12,180
And so he asked me that question, and I realized, "Man, my, my prayers had actually gotten smaller.

42
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:16,420
And my faith was safer.

43
00:03:16,420 --> 00:03:24,220
And what used to be a very passionate, raw faith in God had just become comfortable."

44
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:30,860
Now, when we talk about comfort today, I'm going to tell you right now that comfort alone isn't wrong or bad.

45
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:34,820
It's good to be comfortable, but spiritually, comfort can be very dangerous

46
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:39,460
when it quietly replaces your dependence on God.

47
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:43,460
And so I'm going to show you today from God's word

48
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:51,060
that God doesn't position you for comfort, He positions you for purpose and for His glory.

49
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:58,540
And Esther is going to discover that in the beginning of this week and next week, and my prayer is that you will, too.

50
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:05,260
So would you pray with me? Heavenly Father, we thank You for Your presence, and we thank You for Your word.

51
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:12,180
We pray, God, that You would speak to all of us, God, especially those who may have grown more comfortable.

52
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:20,540
Build our faith that we can take faith steps and faith risks and believe You for big things as we follow You.

53
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:28,420
We pray this in Jesus' name, and everybody said, "Amen." Remember, you prayed for it, if you get it today. Okay?

54
00:04:28,420 --> 00:04:33,300
We are in the Old Testament book of Esther. If you were not here last week,

55
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:38,500
we discovered something very interesting about the book of Esther. Do you remember what it is? Of every book in the Bible,

56
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:47,780
this is the only—what is it? What is it? What is it? It's the only book that doesn't mention God's name directly.

57
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:52,900
Mind-blowing. Every book in the Bible, this is the only one that doesn't mention God's name directly.

58
00:04:52,900 --> 00:05:00,340
But the good news is, God's presence and God's power is found on every single page.

59
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:05,380
Even though His name isn't obvious, His power and presence is obvious.

60
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:10,260
So let's review in case you missed last week. We introduced four big characters last week,

61
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:15,700
the four main characters, I called them the starting lineup. First of all, we have King Xerxes, remember?

62
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:20,940
The king is very well known for throwing big parties, and at a particular party, he got very, very drunk.

63
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:27,540
And King Xerxes had the wife Queen Vashti, and he said, "Hey, go get her.

64
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:32,460
I want to display her to everybody. She's very, very beautiful. I want everybody to see my trophy wife."

65
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:39,380
Essentially. That's not what the Bible says directly, but that's essentially what it says. And the queen, perhaps she was tired of being paraded around

66
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:45,980
or being used or whatever, but on this particular day she said, "No go. It ain't happening. Sorry, king. No, I'm not coming out."

67
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:51,980
And so the king, Xerxes, got angry and banished the queen,

68
00:05:51,980 --> 00:06:00,100
and all the sudden she's no longer there, and the throne is empty. Enter Esther, the book is named after Esther.

69
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:08,340
She was a very ordinary girl, probably a teenager, a Jewish orphan, who then is taken to the palace,

70
00:06:08,340 --> 00:06:16,260
and after a year of events, she actually is chosen to become the queen. She had a cousin, what was his name?

71
00:06:16,260 --> 00:06:22,860
Starts with an M and rhymes with Mordecai? She had a cousin named Mordecai, and Mordecai raised her.

72
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:31,060
He was almost like a dad to her. And he ended up overhearing about an assassination plot. Two guys said, "Let's kill the king."

73
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:37,700
And Mordecai heard about this and told Esther, who was the queen, "Hey, somebody's trying to kill the king." She told the king.

74
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:44,700
Essentially, Mordecai saved the king's life. His faithfulness was recorded in this book,

75
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:51,540
and then his good deed apparently forgotten. God's name was not anywhere.

76
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:58,060
God's plan was everywhere. None of it looked like God was in it,

77
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:02,740
but God was in all of it. Today, we're going to be in chapters 3 and 4,

78
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:08,540
and this is where the music changes. Things get dark.

79
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:18,500
You ever watching the show, and the characters don't know it's about to get scary, but the music changes? And you want to scream,

80
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:26,220
"Don't touch the dead guy! He's not really dead!" Right? This is, this is what's happening here.

81
00:07:26,220 --> 00:07:31,340
The music changes because Esther's now been queen for about four years, and she's starting to finally

82
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:36,460
feel comfortable. She's starting to enjoy the life that she didn't plan.

83
00:07:36,460 --> 00:07:44,140
And honestly, I don't blame her. If you think about it, the poor girl, she lost her parents, she's raised by her cousin,

84
00:07:44,140 --> 00:07:48,940
she's taken against her will into a palace with a bunch of other beautiful women,

85
00:07:48,940 --> 00:07:56,380
and then chosen not for her character, her heart, her intelligence, her personality, but for her beauty,

86
00:07:56,380 --> 00:08:05,260
and she now becomes the queen because of that. Now she's finally starting to feel secure, starting to feel comfortable.

87
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:13,860
And again, comfort alone isn't bad. But God rarely calls us to stay in the places

88
00:08:13,860 --> 00:08:22,460
we're most comfortable. Why? Because comfort doesn't require faith.

89
00:08:22,460 --> 00:08:34,300
And the author to the Hebrews said that without faith, it's impossible to please God. So, right when she starts to get comfortable,

90
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:44,180
the bad guy steps in. His name is Haman. And he's a bad guy.

91
00:08:44,180 --> 00:08:49,180
Our coin looks scary. We made him look scary, 'cause he's the bad guy.

92
00:08:49,180 --> 00:08:55,700
Every great story has a bad guy, think about it. Batman has Joker.

93
00:08:55,700 --> 00:09:00,340
Star Wars, Darth Vader.

94
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:06,180
SpongeBob has Plankton. Esther has Haman.

95
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:12,620
We see this in Esther 3:1. This is the story: Some time later, King Xerxes promoted

96
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:21,060
the bad guy Haman, son of hard word to pronounce, the Agagite,

97
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:28,340
over all the other nobles, making Haman the most powerful official in the kingdom.

98
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:34,700
So you got the king's most powerful, and now you got his most powerful ranking official, and all the royal officials

99
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:44,580
at the king's gate knelt down and paid honor to the bad guy Haman, for the king had commanded this concerning him. But

100
00:09:44,580 --> 00:09:50,420
Esther's cousin, Mordecai, would not kneel down and pay him honor.

101
00:09:50,420 --> 00:09:57,260
What do we see here? Well, we see that everyone bows to Haman except for one guy, Mordecai.

102
00:09:57,260 --> 00:10:00,420
Why didn't he bow? Well, probably because

103
00:10:00,420 --> 00:10:06,660
he was Jewish, and he wouldn't bow to anyone but God. Which shows us the principle

104
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:16,780
that sometimes your greatest act of courage is refusing to do what everyone else is doing.

105
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:20,740
And if you're a follower of Jesus, you actually know that to be true.

106
00:10:20,740 --> 00:10:27,100
You know, sometimes whatever it is, you know, you're trying to follow Jesus in your life, and all of your other friends are getting drunk and partying their brains out,

107
00:10:27,100 --> 00:10:35,460
and even though they're doing it, you say, "Well, I'm not going to do that because Jesus has called me not to be better, but to be different, to be holy and to be set apart."

108
00:10:35,460 --> 00:10:41,260
Or or maybe you don't sleep around when that's considered the absolute norm and everyone expects you to,

109
00:10:41,260 --> 00:10:45,540
but you're not going to, why? Because you have the courage to be different.

110
00:10:45,540 --> 00:10:52,660
Everyone else is gossiping at work and you don't judge them and you're not better than them, but you walk away because you're called to be different.

111
00:10:52,660 --> 00:10:56,380
Different isn't better. Different is different.

112
00:10:56,380 --> 00:11:00,500
If you're the same, you're probably not following Jesus. I tell people all the time, "Don't worry if you're

113
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:04,900
different. Worry if you're not." Sometimes the greatest act of courage

114
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:11,540
is just stepping away when everyone else stays in the wrong place. Mordecai won't bow.

115
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:16,020
Everyone else bows. And he says, "I don't bow to anyone but the true God."

116
00:11:16,020 --> 00:11:24,260
And so Haman, the bad guy, he loses it. I mean, he blows a gasket.

117
00:11:24,260 --> 00:11:32,940
And in some ways, I can relate. Because this guy had everything you'd want. He's powerful, everybody else is bowing,

118
00:11:32,940 --> 00:11:38,420
and one bro doesn't bow. And he falls apart.

119
00:11:38,420 --> 00:11:45,980
That happens to me. Several people say, "Good sermon, Pastor Craig. Good sermon, Pastor Craig. Good sermon, Pastor Craig."

120
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:57,500
I mean, isn't it amazing how one, one

121
00:11:57,500 --> 00:12:03,820
unmet expectation can rob you of the joy of all the other blessings that you already have?

122
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,500
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:51,540
One person says, "That's a dumb sermon, and your shirt didn't look good." And I fall apart. Okay? Whatever.

123
00:12:03,820 --> 00:12:09,860
One guy doesn't bow, and Haman puffs up. Instead of letting it go, he lets it consume him.

124
00:12:09,860 --> 00:12:13,860
"This one guy won't bow down to me." And then it got worse.

125
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:19,940
He discovers Haman is Persian, and he discovers that Mordecai is Jewish.

126
00:12:19,940 --> 00:12:24,820
And the Persian people despised the Jewish people.

127
00:12:24,820 --> 00:12:29,180
Suddenly, it wasn't about one man anymore.

128
00:12:29,180 --> 00:12:35,740
Now Haman, the bad guy, didn't want to just kill Mordecai,

129
00:12:35,740 --> 00:12:44,580
but instead, he wanted to kill every single Jewish person in the entire empire.

130
00:12:44,580 --> 00:12:52,660
Tragic, horrific, horrible. Verse 6 of Esther 3 says this:

131
00:12:52,660 --> 00:12:58,060
"Yet having learned who Mordecai's people were," okay, they're Jewish, "he scorned the idea

132
00:12:58,060 --> 00:13:03,340
of killing only Mordecai. Instead," Haman the bad guy,

133
00:13:03,340 --> 00:13:12,860
"looked for a way to destroy all of Mordecai's people, the Jews, throughout the whole kingdom of Xerxes."

134
00:13:12,860 --> 00:13:20,020
One guy won't bow, and this guy determines, "I'm going to kill them all." So what does Haman do?

135
00:13:20,020 --> 00:13:27,540
He goes to the king. "King," and he tells him a lie. He makes up this story and he tries to manipulate the king.

136
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:33,860
He doesn't say, "King, one man won't bow to me." Instead, what he says is, "King,

137
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:40,940
all these people are dangerous. None of them obeys your laws."

138
00:13:40,940 --> 00:13:49,180
And the king says, "Oh, that's not good." And he believes Haman's lie, and so he gives Haman permission to kill every Jewish person in the empire.

139
00:13:49,180 --> 00:13:51,540
Verse 13 tells us this:

140
00:13:51,540 --> 00:13:58,420
"Letters were sent by couriers to all the king's provinces with instructions to destroy,

141
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:09,780
to kill, to annihilate all Jews, young and old, women and children, in one day." Kill them all.

142
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:14,460
And so the Bible says that Haman, says he cast lots.

143
00:14:14,460 --> 00:14:20,060
I don't know if you know what that means or not, if it means like drawing straws or like rolling dice. He said, "Okay,

144
00:14:20,060 --> 00:14:23,740
what day are we going to destroy them all?" And he cast lots,

145
00:14:23,740 --> 00:14:27,820
and the day ends up on being 11 months later.

146
00:14:27,820 --> 00:14:32,020
So 11 months from now, he's going to destroy all the Jews.

147
00:14:32,020 --> 00:14:36,460
And he thinks to himself, the date was random.

148
00:14:36,460 --> 00:14:40,540
It looks like chance. But what looks like chance

149
00:14:40,540 --> 00:14:47,420
is often God's providence in disguise.

150
00:14:47,420 --> 00:14:50,900
Some of you were here last week, remember what providence is?

151
00:14:50,900 --> 00:14:57,220
That that's when God uses ordinary circumstances to bring about His supernatural will.

152
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:01,500
00:14:57,220 --> 00:15:04,180
That's when the very thing that's going on in your life that you don't understand and wish wasn't there, years later you look back and say, "Oh,

153
00:15:04,180 --> 00:15:09,580
I see how God used something in my life that I didn't expect to do something that only He could do."

154
00:15:09,580 --> 00:15:15,620
Haman thinks, "Okay, it just so happens that 11 months from now, that's the date that we're going to do it." But he didn't know

155
00:15:15,620 --> 00:15:22,460
that God was giving Esther time to discover her purpose when she was in the palace.

156
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:24,940
Because at this time she doesn't know her purpose.

157
00:15:24,940 --> 00:15:30,940
She didn't even know at this point that there was a plan to kill her people. Why?

158
00:15:30,940 --> 00:15:33,780
Because she was comfortable.

159
00:15:33,780 --> 00:15:36,060
Safe.

160
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:42,340
Hidden, still hiding her Jewish identity

161
00:15:42,340 --> 00:15:46,260
while her people were under a death sentence.

162
00:15:46,260 --> 00:15:50,860
Now I want to ask you—a lot of build up, thank you for staying with me—application:

163
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:54,780
How often is that us?

164
00:15:54,780 --> 00:16:01,020
How often, in some area of our lives, are we living in comfort,

165
00:16:01,020 --> 00:16:06,100
unaware of something tragic very nearby?

166
00:16:06,100 --> 00:16:11,540
You see, this is one of the many problems with comfort. Comfort doesn't just make us comfortable, it makes us less aware.

167
00:16:11,540 --> 00:16:21,580
Whenever I find myself more comfortable, I am less aware of people that are hurting very close to me.

168
00:16:21,580 --> 00:16:26,620
When I'm comfortable, I'm less aware of those people who are far from God.

169
00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:32,860
When I'm comfortable, I'm less aware of my need for Him.

170
00:16:32,860 --> 00:16:40,260
And this is why I want to come today, and I hope you receive this as loving, because there's, there is, there is no judgment because

171
00:16:40,260 --> 00:16:44,460
I've been there way too often. For some of you right now, you are comfortable in your faith.

172
00:16:44,460 --> 00:16:49,740
You call yourself a follower of Jesus and it's been a long time since you've done anything that takes faith.

173
00:16:49,740 --> 00:16:55,220
You're comfortable. You know, what we've been pursuing comfort in the name of Jesus, and now we're comfortable.

174
00:16:55,220 --> 00:17:03,820
And so whatever it is, like you're so focused on the next, next vacation, that you don't even notice your teenager is drifting away.

175
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:08,220
You're so busy like scrolling on your phone at night, you don't even realize the person next to you

176
00:17:08,220 --> 00:17:16,740
is desperate to have an intimate conversation with you, but can't because your attention is always somewhere else.

177
00:17:16,740 --> 00:17:22,860
Some people, but we just get so busy enjoying the blessings of God

178
00:17:22,860 --> 00:17:30,220
that we forget that He called us to a life outside of our own comfort and selfishness to be a blessing to other people.

179
00:17:30,220 --> 00:17:34,220
And this was me about a decade in.

180
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:42,340
Pastor Craig. I'd worked so hard. Things were finally starting to go well, and I just thought, "I kind of want a break. I kind of want easy. I kind of want comfort."

181
00:17:42,340 --> 00:17:47,100
And I was, it was like I was so thankful for the fruit, like finally it's there,

182
00:17:47,100 --> 00:17:51,180
I didn't realize that I'd stop living by faith.

183
00:17:51,180 --> 00:17:54,740
And it's easy to do.

184
00:17:54,740 --> 00:18:01,860
Because even though like we believe in God, most of you do, most of you believe in God, but just because you believe in God,

185
00:18:01,860 --> 00:18:07,340
sometimes it seems like life is just happening outside of God.

186
00:18:07,340 --> 00:18:14,780
Just another day, just another day, and another day, and I'm comfortable.

187
00:18:14,780 --> 00:18:21,500
And another day, and I'm comfortable. And it's not that we're rejecting God's calling,

188
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:25,780
we just get distracted from it.

189
00:18:25,780 --> 00:18:32,460
One comfortable day becomes a comfortable week, becomes a comfortable month, becomes a comfortable year,

190
00:18:32,460 --> 00:18:37,700
and then we wake up and realize we built a comfortable life,

191
00:18:37,700 --> 00:18:43,460
and we're no longer living by faith.

192
00:18:43,460 --> 00:18:48,340
And so I want to talk to you, and when I do,

193
00:18:48,340 --> 00:18:53,420
my prayer is that the Holy Spirit will talk through my words to you.

194
00:18:53,420 --> 00:18:58,060
Because I can't, I can't do it right. I don't have the power.

195
00:18:58,060 --> 00:19:03,820
But that those of you that are followers of Jesus, you can hear the Spirit of God speaking to you.

196
00:19:03,820 --> 00:19:08,700
And what I want you to recognize is that you never hear God's word without God having a purpose.

197
